java.py typed a local only from its INITIALIZER (new T(), or a resolved
in-repo method's return type) and never read the declaration one token
to the left, so `OutputStream o = sock.getOutputStream(); o.write(b)`
glued the variable name into the callee (java:external:0-0:o.write). On
guacamole-client that was 315 typed bindings out of ~2,180.

Declared locals, catch parameters, for-each variables, try-with-resources,
explicitly typed lambda parameters, record components, generic and
inline-qualified fields, and fields declared in an ancestor class now
bind the declared type. A constructor initializer still narrows it to
the concrete class; a return-type INFERENCE never overrides a
declaration -- the lookup behind it is name-keyed, and on guacamole it
answered for an enum constant's covariant override and for an anonymous
class's get() reached through a chained receiver, retyping a declared
String as Future. Locals are scoped to their method by a scope stack
keyed on the method's end byte, so an anonymous class body inside the
method closes its own scope and not the enclosing one; before, a local
`File f` in one method typed a sibling method's `Socket f` FIELD.

The module slot for a declared type: the import, an inline FQN, a nested
type on an imported outer, a wildcard package, or -- for the closed list
of java.lang public types only, since a forgotten `import
java.io.InputStream` must not become java.lang.InputStream -- the JLS
7.3 implicit import. `Runtime rt = Runtime.getRuntime(); rt.exec(cmd)`
reaches the catalogued subprocess sink.

WI-kuroj's method_return_type_registry was built in analyze_java and
never passed to _extract_edges (write-only; WI-doluf's Go Defect 1). It
is now the one interface a return type is read through: key
<Owner>.<method> (project class bare, library owner qualified), value
qualified through the DECLARING file's imports or a bare project class --
the shape WI-lalot's library rows will feed. Consulted at the assignment,
typed-receiver and chained sites; ADR-0006's Java "Chained" column flips.

